37 1 Introduction
38 Meeting an ever increasing demand for the food, feed, and fuel and managing
39 waste, especially the organic waste, has become a major global challenge. The situation is
40 projected to be aggravated with the rapidly increasing global population, which is
41 estimated to increase from 7.3 billion in 2015 to 9.7 billion in 2050 [1]. Currently, about
42 one-third of food produced, which is equivalent to 1.3 billion metric tons is wasted or lost
43 and has significant environmental (i.e., greenhouse gas (GHGs) emissions) and economic
44 footprints [2]. For example, in India alone, the postharvest loss of agricultural produce is
45 estimated to be about 92 million tons (Mton) per year [3]. Without considering the land
46 use change, the annual GHGs emissions equivalent of food wastage is estimated to be 3.3
47 billion metric tons of CO2 equivalent GHGs [2]. Similarly, the economic footprint of the
48 food produced but not consumed accounts for the loss of nearly $750 billion per year [2].
49 Additionally, waste management, especially in developing and underdeveloped countries,
50 has become a serious issue. The management of organic wastes, among different wastes,
51 is more challenging due to its bulky nature and rapid degradability [4]. The current organic
52 wastes management practices, namely land fill and waste treatment/stabilization via
53 anaerobic digestion and composting to meet environmental regulations, are not only
54 costly but also have adverse impacts on environment such as ground and surface water
55 contaminations and GHGs emissions among others [4, 5].
56 On the other hand, both rapid increase in global population and associated
57 affluence are believed to have caused significant increase in demand for food, feed, and


4
58 fuel, and waste generation [5]. For example, global energy demand is estimated to
59 increase from 524 Quadrillion Btu (QBtu) in 2010 to 820 QBtu by 2040, a 56% increase
60 compared to 2010 [6]. Similarly, the global demands for food and animal products are
61 projected to increase by 70-100% [7] and 50-70% [8], respectively, by 2050. To cope up
62 with the demand for animal products, a substantial increase in nutritious animal
63 feedstuffs is needed. On one hand, the production of conventional feedstuffs such as
64 soybean meal is reported as the major contributor to land occupation, climate change,
65 and water and energy consumption [9, 10]. Additionally, such conventional animal
66 feedstuffs are not only limited in supply but also are becoming more expensive over the
67 years. On the other hand, the growing concern of climate change, which is expected to
68 bring extreme climate variability (e.g., long drought, heavy winter storm, and occurrence
69 of frequent floods among others), the situation is likely to aggravate further the food
70 security situation due to its impact on agriculture-based food and feed production
71 systems [11]. Moreover, there is already a strong competition for resources such as food,
72 feed, and biofuel productions, and the competition is projected to be even fiercer with
73 increased demand for food, feed, and fuel. Thus, there is a pressing need for identifying
74 and exploring the potential of alternative non-conventional sources of food, feed, and
75 fuel, which are economically viable, environmentally friendly, and socially acceptable.
76 Black soldier fly (BSF) (Hermetia illucens), a detritivorous insect belonging to order
77 Diptera and family Stratiomyidae, is native to tropical, subtropical, and warm temperate
78 zone of America [12]. The insect is now distributed, due to global trade and business, in


5
79 tropical to warm temperate regions in Europe [13], North [14] and South America,
80 Australia [15], and Asia [16, 17]. Due to its widespread distribution and ease with which it
81 can be maintained in a colony [14], there is substantial global interest in the mass
82 production of the BSF as a means to produce protein [15].
83 The BSF lifecycle consists of four stages, namely; egg, larvae, pupae, and adult.
84 Adults typically mate for two days after emergence [18]. Females lay a single clutch of
85 eggs for two days after mating [18] and resulting eggs hatch in approximately four days
86 [12]. Within natural environments (e.g., livestock operations), eggs are deposited in cracks
87 and crevices near to food sources [19]. Larvae pass through five instars. Larvae are quite
88 omnivorous, as they feed on a variety of materials ranging from animal [20] and human
89 feces [21], kitchen waste [22] to vertebrate remains (e.g., decomposing swine carcasses)
90 [23]. Depending on the size of the larvae, type of the substrate available, and
91 environmental conditions (e.g., moisture, temperature, and air supply), the larvae
92 consume from 25-500 mg of organic matter per larva per day [24]. Similarly, depending
93 on the substrate type, the larvae are reported to reduce the waste by about 39% (pig
94 manure) [25] and 50% (chicken manure) [26] to 68% (municipal organic waste) [27] and
95 have a food conversion ratio (FCR) of about 10 to 15 [25, 26, 27]. The larval stage is
96 usually 14 days or longer depending on availability of food [12], and appropriate
97 environmental conditions [28, 29]. During the later larval stage; the stage prior to
98 pupation termed as prepupae [26], larvae get rid of their digestive track and migrate away
99 from their food sources in search of dry and protected place to pupate [26]. Since adults


6
100 are non-feeding, BSF larvae consume organic matter as much as possible and store fat
101 and protein in their body to support their metabolism during pupal and adult stages [25].
102 By using a specially designed bioreactor, the typical migrating behavior of prepupae can
103 be exploited for self-harvesting of prepupae [26] for extracting fat and protein for value
104 added products generation. The pupation stage usually lasts for two weeks under ideal
105 environmental conditions [12, 30, 31].
106 In the context of growing demand for food, feed, and fuel, as well as a need for
107 managing organic wastes, the use of insect, to efficiently convert organic wastes into fuel,
108 food, and feed appears to be innovative and promising. Thus, this study aims to farm a
109 unique insect, the BSF on such organic waste as a novel strategy in managing organic
110 wastes and producing high-value fat- and protein-rich insect biomass. Thereafter, the
111 produced fat- and protein-rich insect biomass, in downstream processing, can be
112 fractionated into fat (i.e., raw material for biodiesel production) and protein-rich insect
113 meal (for animal feed applications). The specific objectives of this study were to (i)
114 characterize fatty acids profile of the insect fat as a potential substrate for biodiesel
115 production, and (ii) analyze the proximate composition and amino acids profile insect
116 biomass and defatted insect meal for animal feed application.


