117 2 Materials and methods
118 2.1 Black soldier fly rearing and oil extraction
119 BSF was grown in the food wastes collected from school cafeteria (Clemson
120 University, Clemson, SC USA and Pearl City High School, Honolulu, HI USA) by using
121 specially designed reactor for self-harvesting of prepupae. The schematic of bioconversion
122 of food waste to biofuel and animal feed is presented in Figure 1. Harvested larvae were
123 dried using conveyer oven (Despatch Oven Co., Minneapolis, MN USA) at 60 °C to the
124 moisture content of around 5-8%. The dried BSF prepupae grown in Clemson University
125 and Pearl City High School were mixed to make a composite sample. The mixed dried BSF
126 prepupae were fractionated into crude oil and cake/meal using both mechanical and
127 chemical means. Mechanical extraction was conducted using a lab-scale Taby Press Type
128 20 (Skeppsta Maskin AB, Sweden). The extracted crude oil was collected, centrifuged, and
129 the mass of the supernatant oil was recorded. The pressed cake following mechanical
130 extraction was further chemically extracted using a Soxhlet apparatus (Ace Glass
131 Incorporated, NJ USA) for four hours using petroleum ether as a solvent. Afterwards,
132 solvent was removed using a rotary evaporator (Buchi, Flawil, Switzerland) at 50 °C. BSF
133 prepupae, pressed cake, and pressed and solvent extracted meal (termed as “meal”
134 hereafter) were sent to the Department of Aquatic Feeds and Nutrition at the Oceanic
135 Institute (Waimanalo, HI, USA) for complete characterization (i.e. fatty acids composition
136 and nutritional profile).


