140 2.2 Analytical methods
141 The nutritional profile of the BSF prepupae, pressed cake, and meal were
142 characterized following the Association of Official Analytical Chemists (AOAC) standard
143 procedures [32] with specific methods as follows: dry matter (DM) (oven drying at 105 °C
144 to constant weight), ash (ignition at 550 °C in an electric furnace, AOAC 942.05), crude
145 fiber (AOAC 962.09), crude protein by determining N by dry combustion using LECO
146 analyzer (LECO FP-528 Nitrogen determiner, Leco Corp., St. Joseph, MI) (AOAC 976.05,
147 crude protein = N × 6.25), crude lipid (AOAC 920.39; using ether extraction (ASE-200
148 apparatus), and carbohydrate by subtracting the crude protein, crude lipid and ash
149 contents from total dry matter. Gross energy content was determined using an oxygen
150 bomb calorimeter (Parr Bomb Calorimeter 6200, Parr Instrument Co., Moline, IL). Amino
151 acids (both essential and non-essential) was determined using High Performance Liquid
152 Chromatography (Agilent 1200, Agilent Technologies Inc., Santa Clara, CA) using AOAC
153 method no 982.30 E (a,b,c). Fatty acids including EPA and DHA were analyzed using a gas
154 chromatograph (Varian 3800 GC; Varian Analytical Instrument, Walnut Creek, CA)
155 equipped with a flame ionization detector (AOAC 996.06;). During fatty acid profile


9
156 analysis, the C12:0 in BSF prepupae derived oil was determined by assigning the peak
157 before C14:0 as a peak for C12:0 by based on literature review [33, 34, 35].
158 2.3 Statistical analyses
159 The experiments and sample analyses for all important parameters discussed
160 above were performed in triplicates. The statistical analysis was conducted using
161 Statistical Analysis System (SAS) software (SAS 9.2, SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC USA). The
162 statistical differences were determined by a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA)
163 followed by Tukey’s T-test with a threshold value (α) of 0.05.
164 3 Results and discussion
165 3.1 Yield and fatty acids composition of BSF derived oil
166 The mechanical pressing of 100 g of prepupae resulted about 15-20 g of crude oil
167 and 80-85 g of pressed cake (data not presented). The mechanical pressing extracted
168 nearly 40% of oil content in the insect biomass. The solvent extraction of the pressed and
169 milled BSF prepupae, however, removed about 90% of total oil content in the BSF
170 prepupae.
171 The low oil yield during mechanical pressing could be mainly because of the low
172 crude fiber content (~10%) in BSF prepupae, which is not sufficient to create enough back
173 pressure during pressing. The low oil yield during pressing could also be because of the
174 equipment used in this study. The Taby Press Type 20 (Skeppsta Maskin AB, Sweden) used


10
175 in this study was a lab scale/kitchen press mainly designed for extracting oil from oil crops
176 (usually rich in fiber content) and was not optimized for the feedstock such as insect
177 biomass. The large scale mechanical press especially designed for extracting oil from low
178 fiber containing feedstocks could improve the oil extraction from BSF prepupae. Further,
179 the extraction efficiency of BSF prepupae by mechanical means could potentially be
180 improved by co-pressing BSF prepupae with fiber rich substrates such as sunflower seeds
181 and macadamia nuts. Since fatty acid composition varies with substrate (e.g., BSF derived
182 oil hag high saturated fatty acids (~70%) and low in polyunsaturated fatty acids (~12%)
183 while sunflower oil is rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids (~65%) and poor in saturated
184 fatty acids (~10%)) (Table 1), such co-extraction could improve not only the oil extraction
185 efficiency, but also the fatty acid profile in the extracted oil for high quality biodiesel
186 production.
187 3.2 Fatty acid composition
188 The composition of the fatty acids derived from BSF is summarized in Table 2. It
189 was found that lauric (C12:0), palmitic (C16:0), and oleic (C18:1) acids were the most
190 dominating fatty acids in the oil derived from BSF prepupae. The relative percentage of
191 the lauric, palmitic, and oleic acids were about 45, 14, and 12%, respectively, followed by
192 linoleic (C16:2) (10%) and myristic (C14:0) (8%) acids. The relative concentration of the
193 saturated, monounsaturated, and polyunsaturated fatty acids in the BSF derived oil were
194 about 70, 15, and 13% of total fatty acids, respectively (Table 2). The relative dominance
195 of lauric, palmitic, and oleic acids in the BSF prepupae derived oil were also reported by


11
196 others [33, 34, 35]. The dominance of the lauric acid in BSF prepupae derived oil is unique
197 compared to the oil derived from the crops which is most commonly used for biodiesel
198 production (e.g., soybean, sunflower, and palm) [36]. The relative concentration of the
199 medium chain saturated fatty acids (C12:0 ‒ C16:0) in the BSF prepupae derived oil (67%
200 of total fatty acids) is higher compared to the soybean (11% of total fatty acids) and palm
201 oil (37% of total fatty acids) (Table 2). In addition, the relative concentration of
202 unsaturated (i.e., mono- and poly-unsaturated) fatty acids in the oil derived from BSF
203 prepupae (28% of total fatty acids) is less than in the soybean oil (85%) and palm oil
204 (55%). The fatty acids composition of the substrate (i.e., oil) has been stated to have
205 significant effect on the quality of the produced biodiesel [36]. The biodiesel production
206 process from vegetable oils and animal fats has been discussed elsewhere [37, 38]. In
207 general, high concentrations of long chain saturated fatty acids in the substrate have been
208 reported to produce biodiesel with poor cold flow property (i.e., low cold plugging point).
209 Similarly, the biodiesel derived from the oil rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids have been
210 found to have poor oxidative stability, which means the low storability of the produced
211 biodiesel [36]. Thus, the relatively high concentration of medium chain saturated fatty
212 acids (i.e., 67%) and low concentration of the polyunsaturated fatty acids (i.e., 13%)
213 makes the BSF prepupae derived oil potentially an ideal substrate for producing high
214 quality biodiesel (i.e., lower viscosity and higher oxidative stability). Additionally, Li et al.
215 [33] mentioned that the important biofuel properties, namely density, viscosity, flash


12
216 point, and Cetane index, of biodiesel derived from BSF larva were within the
217 recommended range of international standard of biodiesel.
