236 The proximate composition of BSF prepupae obtained in this study was within the range
237 of reported studies [25, 35]. The proximate composition of BSF prepupae, especially crude
238 protein and crude fat contents, however, differs with the substrate type and growth stage
239 (e.g., larvae, prepupae, and pupae) [25, 40]. When BSF larvae were fed with cow manure
240 and mixture of cow manure and fish offal at different ratios, St-Hilaire et al. [40] observed
241 variation in not only the total lipid content but also in the composition of fatty acids. The
242 authors reported an increase in lipid content from 21% (fed with cow manure) to 30% as
243 well as substantial enrichment (2.5–3.8% of total lipid) of omega-3 fatty acids in the
244 prepupae fed with mixture of fish offal and cow manure. The majority of the lipid stored
245 in the insect body is triglyceride, which is synthesized from carbohydrates, fatty acids, or
246 proteins [41, 42]. Diglyceride is the immediate precursor for triglyceride formation. The
247 esterification of diglyceride in the presence of diacylglycerol acyltransferase as a catalyst
248 leads to the formation of triglyceride [42, 43]. The conversion of carbohydrates present in
249 insect diet into lipid in the insect fat body has been reported elsewhere [42, 44, 45]. The
250 capacity of fat body for lipid synthesis from carbohydrate is higher than for glycogen
251 synthesis, thus insect fat body contains higher amount of lipid than glycogen [42].
252 The crude protein content of the BSF prepupae, pressed cake, and meal were
253 compared with the most common protein source in animal feeds such as soybean meal
254 and fishmeal. The crude protein content of the BSF prepupae meal (63.9%) was higher
255 than crude protein content of solvent extracted soybean meal (43.9%) and was


14
256 comparable with the crude protein content of fish meal (63.2%). The crude protein
257 content of BSF prepupae pressed cake (53.1%), however, was in between crude protein
258 content of soybean meal and fishmeal (Table 1). Though the BSF prepupae, pressed cake,
259 and meal are treated as protein source, the energy content of the BSF prepupae, pressed
260 cake, and meal (i.e., 5752, 5233, and 4614 Kcal/kg DM, respectively) are higher than the
261 reference protein sources (i.e., 4257 and 4496 Kcal/kg DM for soybean meal and fish
262 meal, respectively). The energy content of the BSF prepupae, pressed cake, and meal are
263 comparable or higher than the most common energy source in animal diets such as corn
264 (4508 Kcal/kg DM) and wheat (4063 Kcal/kg DM). Additionally, the crude fiber content in
265 BSF prepupae (10.1%), pressed cake (10.9%), and meal (13.2%) were higher than the
266 reference protein sources (6.6% in soybean meal and 0.2% in fishmeal), but were in range
267 with reference energy sources (2.4 and 1.7% in corn and wheat, respectively). Thus BSF
268 prepupae, pressed cake, and meal have potential to serve as both protein and energy
269 source in animal diets.
270 The removal of crude fat from BSF prepupae not only improved the crude protein
271 content of the pressed cake and meal but also the removal of crude fat from high fat
272 containing feedstuffs is essential for efficient mixing of feed ingredients while processing
273 animal feeds. Further, the fat removal is necessary to improve the storability, especially
274 the oxidative stability, of the feed. In addition, fat removal from the BSF prepupae is
275 believed to increase the protein digestibility of the BSF prepupae derived feed.


